From aabd3c136917184862f5119bcb28075f581eeb1f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FiyeroT <me@fiyero.xyz>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 21:15:48 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 007/125] dns: fall back to the provider's DNS when a server
 reached through the WAN fails

With SING_BOX_DNS_FALLBACK naming a resolv.conf (podkop-engine sets
/tmp/resolv.conf.d/resolv.conf.auto when podkop is installed), a query
that a server fails to answer (no answer in 3.5 s, refused or reset
connection, TLS or HTTP error) is asked of the provider's DNS servers
listed there, from a fresh socket through the server's own dialer; a
SERVFAIL is asked there too. After two failures in a row the server is
set aside: queries go straight to the provider and a copy of one query
checks the server in the background (after 30 s, 30 s, 60 s and so on up
to every 5 minutes); the first normal answer brings it back. Provider
answers keep a TTL of at most 60 s. If the provider fails to answer
twice in a row, the fallback is off for that server for a minute, then
the provider is tried again; a query given up by its caller does not
count. Provider queries have their
own pool of 256 sockets: a burst of queries waiting for a silent server
must not keep them from the provider. NXDOMAIN is an answer and is not
asked again.

Which servers qualify is decided once, from the configuration: a plain
socket (no detour, or a detour to a direct outbound) without dial fields
that send it another way (bind_interface, routing_mark, bind address,
netns, or any field not known to leave the route alone), to a public
address or a domain name. Servers behind proxies, outbound groups or
bound interfaces (VPN) and local or private resolvers (127.0.0.0/8,
RFC 1918, CGNAT, link-local, ULA) behave exactly as before. Routes of the
system are not looked at.

The logic lives in dns/client_fallback.go. The client calls it at both
exchange points: the synchronous one and the asynchronous one that
queries hijacked from clients take (servers without the fallback keep
the transport's own asynchronous exchange). The transport adapter keeps
the per-server state and the direct outbound exposes its dial fields.

Tested with podkop 0.7.22 on OpenWrt 24.10 and 25.12, 17 scenarios (those
of 1.13 plus DoT after 25 s idle): the same behaviour as in 1.13, with
hijacked queries on the asynchronous path. With the server silent under
200 queries per second the provider answered 12000 of 12000 in each of 3
rounds; sockets, goroutines and memory return to the baseline after every
load phase; CPU of the healthy path is the same with the fallback on and
off.
